Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply one is:
```json
{"a": 1}
{"b": 2}
```
{"c": 3}
Reply two is:
```json
{"a": 1}
{"b": 4}
```
{"c": 3}
For reply one the rule requires the answer {"b": 2}, because the fence holds two objects and the last one inside it is {"b": 2}. For reply two the rule requires {"b": 4}, because the last object inside the fence is {"b": 4}. The check, however, takes the whole fenced span as one candidate; that span is two objects separated by a newline, which is not a valid JSON object, so the fenced pool is skipped and the check falls through to the top-level pool, whose last candidate is {"c": 3} in both replies. Therefore the check gives the same answer, {"c": 3}, to both. The conjecture would be false if recover_json_object returned {"b": 2} for reply one and {"b": 4} for reply two, or any different answers for the two replies.

